= Severing's Ban

In May, Carl Severing, the Social Democratic interior minister of Prussia, bans the proletarian hundreds throughout Prussia: in Berlin, the Ruhr, Halle and Hamburg's Prussian suburbs. The police are told to break up their drills and confiscate their arms. In Saxony and Thuringia, where the governments are friendlier, the hundreds are left alone.

At the same time the Ruhr explodes. The strike wages paid from Berlin are worthless by the time they are paid, and in the last week of May the miners and metalworkers of Gelsenkirchen, Dortmund and Bochum strike for wages that keep up with prices. Our members lead many of the strike committees.

- @defy: Defy the ban: the hundreds march in Prussia.
- @saxony: Obey in Prussia, and build the hundreds in Saxony and Thuringia.
- @strikes: Put the party behind the Ruhr strikes for wages.

